<section class="accordion-section section-width--thin">
  <h2 style="margin-bottom: 48px">[ Accordion - Component ]</h2>
  <p>
    Accordions can be used to tuck away lengthy info. They’re often used for FAQ
    sections. The
    <code>accordion.njk</code> component comes with the following configs:
  </p>
  <br />
  <br />

  <ul class="classic-list">
    <li>
      <code>id</code> a string, unique identifier if there’s more than one
      accordion
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>icon</code> an optional string, the path to the icon used for the
      accordion arrow. Default is an empty string (no icon)
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>plus</code> a boolean to use a plus sign (html markup) instad of an
      icon. Defaults to
      <code>false</code>
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>single_open</code> a boolean to only allow one accordion open at a
      time. Defaults to
      <code>false</code>
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>toggle_all</code> a boolean to show a button that opens or closes
      all accordion items at once. Requires <code>single_open</code> to be
      <code>false</code>. Defaults to <code>false</code>. Optionally override
      the button label via <code>toggle_all_text_open</code> and
      <code>toggle_all_text_close</code>, which default to
      <code>Open All</code> and <code>Close All</code>
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>items</code> an object that takes in <code>title</code> and
      <code>content</code> both of which are strings (content is 'safe' for
      markup) — used for the accordion title and message within. You also have
      access to <code>content_start</code> if using
      <code>skip-to-content</code> util in the header.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <br />
  <br />

  <p style="display: block; margin-bottom: 64px">
    Accordion with <code>icon</code> config and <code>single_open</code> set to
    true.
  </p>
  <!-- prettier-ignore -->
  {% set accordion = library.accordion.accordion_1 %}
  {% include '../_includes/components/accordion.njk' %}

  <br /><br /><br /><br />

  <p style="display: block; margin-bottom: 64px">
    Accordion with <code>plus</code> and <code>toggle_all</code> configs
  </p>
  <!-- prettier-ignore -->
  {% set accordion = library.accordion.accordion_2 %}
  {% include '../_includes/components/accordion.njk' %}
</section>

<section class="library-section">
  <h2>[ Card Gnomon - Component ]</h2>
  <p>
    A square card with one or more rectangular notches cut from its corners or
    sides (a “gnomon”), drawn with an inline SVG stroke so the border color can
    transition on <code>:hover</code> — see
    <a
      class="classic-link tab-element-page"
      href="https://landonorris.com/"
      target="_blank"
    >
      landonorris.com </a
    >, “Helmets Hall of Fame”, for the reference. The
    <code>card-gnomon.njk</code> component comes with the following configs:
  </p>
  <br />
  <br />

  <ul class="classic-list">
    <li>
      <code>cutouts</code> an array of <code>{ from, text }</code> objects — one
      entry per notch. <code>from</code> is a corner (<code>top-left</code>,
      <code>top-right</code>, <code>bottom-left</code>,
      <code>bottom-right</code>) or the middle of a side (<code>top</code>,
      <code>right</code>, <code>bottom</code>, <code>left</code>);
      <code>text</code> is an optional string rendered inside that notch (sized
      to exactly fill it, centered) — e.g. a number or short label, like the
      “Helmets Hall of Fame” cards on the reference site. Any number of cutouts
      can be combined (e.g. <code>top-right</code> + <code>bottom-left</code>) —
      if two land on the same edge and would overlap, <code>depth</code>/<code
        >length</code
      >
      are automatically shrunk together so they never collide. Defaults to a
      single <code>top-right</code> cutout with no text
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>depth</code> a number, 0–100, how far every cutout reaches into the
      card as a % of its side — shared by all cutouts. Defaults to
      <code>28</code>
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>length</code> a number, 0–100, how far every cutout runs along its
      edge, as a % of the card's side — for a corner, split across the two edges
      meeting there; for a side, centered on that edge. Shared by all cutouts.
      Defaults to <code>28</code>
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>radius</code> a number, 0–50, the corner curve as a % of the card's
      side — rounds every corner, including every cutout's. Clamped so it can
      never exceed half of the shape's shortest edge. Defaults to <code>6</code>
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>angle</code> a number, 45–90deg, tilts every cutout's inner
      corner(s). <code>90</code> (default) is a square step; <code>45</code>
      slides it into a single diagonal cut — an exact 45° angle when
      <code>depth</code> equals <code>length</code>. Pair it with a larger
      <code>radius</code> for the landonorris.com look: their cutout isn't a
      curved step or a sharp chamfer, it's a straight diagonal (~51°) with each
      end rounded — the border runs straight, curves, then continues straight,
      rather than smoothly bending the whole way. In practice, keep it in the
      75–85 range — much past 55 the length edge shrinks so far that
      <code>radius</code> rounds the whole notch into one blobby curve instead
      of a crisp diagonal
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>border_width</code> a number in px, the stroke width. Defaults to
      <code>2</code>
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>img</code> an object with <code>src</code>, <code>alt</code> and an
      optional <code>eager</code> boolean (loads eagerly instead of lazy). This
      is the primary use case — a single image filling the card
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>content</code> an optional string (rendered 'safe', so it can be
      markup) — use this instead of, or alongside, <code>img</code> when the
      card needs to hold something other than a single image
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>class</code> an optional string, extra class(es) on the root element
    </li>
  </ul>

  <br />
  <br />

  <p style="display: block; margin-bottom: 64px">
    Default cutout (<code>top-right</code>) with an image and text.
  </p>
  <!-- prettier-ignore -->
  {% set card_gnomon = ({
    img: { src: '/static/img/img-sample-1.jpg', alt: 'Sample card image' },
    cutouts: [ { from: 'top-right', text: 'Sample 01' } ]
  }) %}
  {% include "../_includes/components/card-gnomon.njk" %}

  <br /><br /><br /><br />

  <p style="display: block; margin-bottom: 64px">
    <code>cutouts: [{ from: 'bottom-right' }]</code> with a deeper/longer cut
    and raw <code>content</code> instead of an image.
  </p>
  <!-- prettier-ignore -->
  {% set card_gnomon = ({
    cutouts: [ { from: 'bottom-right', text: 'Keep angle near 90' } ],
    depth: 12,
    length: 48,
    angle: 80,
    radius: 4,
    content: '
  <p>This uses an angle of 80</p>
  ' }) %} {% include "../_includes/components/card-gnomon.njk" %}

  <br /><br /><br /><br />

  <p style="display: block; margin-bottom: 64px">
    <code>cutouts: [{ from: 'bottom' }]</code> — a cutout centered on a side
    instead of a corner.
  </p>
  <!-- prettier-ignore -->
  {% set card_gnomon = ({
    cutouts: [ { from: 'bottom', text: 'View more' } ],
    depth: 10,
    length: 32,
    angle: 75,
    radius: 2,
    img: { src: '/static/img/img-sample-1.jpg', alt: 'Sample card image' }
  }) %}
  {% include "../_includes/components/card-gnomon.njk" %}

  <br /><br /><br /><br />

  <p style="display: block; margin-bottom: 64px">
    Two cutouts on opposite corners — any number of entries in
    <code>cutouts</code> can be combined.
  </p>
  <!-- prettier-ignore -->
  {% set card_gnomon = ({
    cutouts: [
      { from: 'top-right', text: 'Sample 01' },
      { from: 'bottom-left', text: 'Sample 02' }
    ],
    depth: 10,
    length: 32,
    angle: 80,
    radius: 2,
    img: { src: '/static/img/img-sample-1.jpg', alt: 'Sample card image' }
  }) %}
  {% include "../_includes/components/card-gnomon.njk" %}
</section>

